# .dockerignore for Tessera.Host container build context.
#
# Goal: minimum bytes crossing the docker build context boundary.
# Anything not strictly required by `dotnet restore` + `dotnet publish`
# of Tessera.Host belongs here.
#
# Rules:
#   - Keep bin/ obj/ out — incremental caches shouldn't be in the build
#     context; the Dockerfile uses BuildKit cache mounts for NuGet anyway.
#   - Keep test projects EXCEPT the two csproj manifests we need for restore
#     (the Dockerfile already copies those before the . -> COPY . .).
#   - Keep web/ out — frontend deferred to a later lane.
#   - Keep .git/ docs/ infra/ out — host build doesn't need them.

# Git metadata (large on big repos, useless to the SDK layer)
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# Build outputs / caches
**/bin
**/obj
**/out
**/TestResults
**/coverage*

# Frontend — separate deployment lane (later)
web/
node_modules/
bun.lock
bun.lockb

# Docs — not consumed by host build
.agents/
docs/
*.md
LICENSE*
.dockerignore
.editorconfig

# IDE / OS noise
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Source generator caches (regenerated on build)
**/Generated/

# Local config / secrets — never bake into images
tessera.toml
tessera.local.toml
.env
.env.local
secrets/

# Module test projects (Host.UnitTests still needed for parallel
# type-resolution during full solution restore — keep those csprojs
# only). The Dockerfile re-copies the specific csprojs it needs before
# this ignore line is reached; this line filters the wholesale
# `COPY . .` so the final layer doesn't carry test source.
tests/
